 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcZzNIgv074/",
 "title": "How to Know If Your Manager Is On Your Side",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "Microsoft marketing leader Simon shares key signals that show whether a manager is genuinely supporting your career growth. Supportive managers actively listen, provide specific constructive feedback, and stay transparent, while unsupportive managers offer vague promises without action.",
 "key_points": [
  "Green Flag 1: They genuinely listen, ask questions, and lean in to understand what you want from your career.",
  "Green Flag 2: They provide specific, constructive coaching (e.g., highlighting areas needing exposure) instead of vague compliments like 'you're doing great.'",
  "Green Flag 3: They do not bluff; they exhibit high self-awareness, admit what they do not know, and give clear timelines and expectations.",
  "Red Flag: They are wishy-washy, beat around the bush, or give false hope to retain you without delivering.",
  "Observe whether your manager's actions line up with their words and commitments; if not, prepare a Plan B and Plan C."
 ],
 "content": "Post Text: ✨Comment LEARN to access my Career Growth Academy. It’s no surprise that a manager can either elevate your career or hold you back. After working with more than a dozen managers throughout my career, I’ve learned to recognize the green flags - and the red ones. The signs that tell you whether your manager will truly advocate for you, create opportunities for you, and support your growth. In this video, I break down 3 green flags and the 1 red flag to watch for. #careergrowth #careerdevelopment #careeradvice #career Transcript: How to know if your manager is actually on your side at work. I've had over 12 plus managers and I've picked up green flags and red flags, signals that tell you whether your manager is good for you or bad for you. And if you're new here, I'm Simon, a marketing leader at Microsoft. I was talking to someone named Alex recently on a coaching call. He had spoken to his manager a few times about his future, like getting promoted, but every conversation felt unclear. And he asked me, what signals should I actually be looking for? And how do I know if my manager is on my side? Here's what I told him. Number one, they genuinely listen. They ask questions to understand you and actually try to understand what you want from your career. Number two, they engage with your specific situation, not generic feedback like 'You're doing great,' but constructive feedback like 'Here's what you need to work on. You need more exposure here. Let me help you.' And number three, probably the biggest one, they are highly self-aware, admit what they don't know, and offer a feeling of support. You leave knowing where you stand, what you need to work on, and a rough timeline. Now, as for the red flags, if your manager is wishy-washy and beating around the bush, you need to rethink. And I've seen this play out before, not with me, but with some of my friends. Some managers might purposely sell you to retain you and give you false hope. They kind of amp everything up. Easiest way to know is to observe if their actions line up with their words and commitment to you. If not, you need to think about your plan B and plan C. A good manager might not always give you the answer you want, but they should help you understand where you stand without overselling. If you want to learn all the soft and hard skills to win at work, I want you to join my community. Give this video a heart and I will see you in the next video. On-Screen Text: How to know if your manager is on your side (green and red flags); I've had over 12 plus managers; Some managers light the way up. Some leave you in the dark. Learn to read the flags before you follow. You can see your next step / You can't; #1 They listen and LEAN in; #2 They coach and get specific; #3 They don't bluff; All talk. Foggy. Moving goalpost; Comment LEARN to join my academy today! Communicate and work like a leader.",
